module test.claim.declared_type_kernel_inhabitance_witness_test

// A PLAIN KERNEL VALUE INHABITS NOTHING, AND SEVEN OF FOURTEEN DECLARED-TYPE POSITIONS ADMITTED IT.
//
// DESIGN section 4b puts `values inhabit declared types` in the ordinary compiler floor, beside
// name resolution and exact-bijection binding, and calls a failure there a below-baseline safety
// regression rather than a rung on the ladder. The census enumerated the declared-type positions
// FROM THE GRAMMAR -- every call site of `parse_type_expr` in `v1.compiler.parse`, fourteen of them
// -- and measured seven accepting an `Int` where a coproduct is declared, each with a reachability
// control (an undefined name at the same position) that refused, so the silence is a measurement
// that no judgment runs there and not a position inference never reached.
//
// This file is the executing evidence for the positions this change closes. Measured by execution
// on the pre-wall binary, each of these fixtures compiled with `0 diagnostics` and EMITTED:
//
//   fn declared return       fn p1() -> KOuter { 5 }              0 diagnostics, 9 files emitted
//   module-scope data        data p2: KOuter = 5                  0 diagnostics, 9 files emitted
//   annotated let            let x: KOuter = 5                    0 diagnostics, 9 files emitted
//   container element        data p4: List<KOuter> = [5]          0 diagnostics, 9 files emitted
//
// WHY THE RELATION IS NOT NEW AND THAT IS THE POINT. `kernel_value_declared_type_mismatch` in
// `v1.compiler.infer` already decided exactly this question, and already refused
// `KHold \{ subject: 5 \}` at the record-literal field seam. One position of fourteen consulted it.
// So what is asserted here is not that a judgment was invented but that it is now performed where a
// declared type is written, which is why `kernel_in_named_field_still_refuses` below is enrolled:
// it is the position that was ALREADY closed, and it is the control proving the relation this
// change reuses is the same one, not a second authority beside it.
//
// THE POSITIVE CONTROLS ARE NOT COSMETIC. Without them a compiler that refused every declaration
// would satisfy every red above, and the reds would measure the mechanism rather than the
// distinction. `correct_value_at_every_closed_position_still_compiles` carries the conforming
// spelling at all four positions in one module, so a refusal anywhere in the set fails it.
//
// THE COVERAGE STANDING IS PER SPELLING, NOT PER POSITION, and this sentence is the narrowing that
// makes the rest of this header read at the scope it actually measures. Every probe above declares
// its type by a DIRECT name, so what they establish is "a kernel value is refused at a DIRECTLY
// named declared type" at those positions. They do NOT establish "a kernel value is refused at that
// declared type", because a reader who takes the second reading has silently generalised over a
// variable the census never varied: the SPELLING of the declared type. Under a one-hop alias the
// second reading was false, at every position that consults the relation without peeling first, and
// the alias section below is what makes it true. THIS IS NOT A CORRECTION OF ANYTHING ABOVE. Each
// claim is true of the position it tests, and its author had no reason to think "declared type
// name" and "declared type name via an alias" were two questions -- nobody knew they were until
// 2026-08-27. The distinction is recorded here because the carrier that answers "is this seam
// covered?" must not return a correct yes that licenses an unsound conclusion, which is exactly how
// this defect survived.
//
// WHAT IS NOT ASSERTED HERE, stated rather than implied covered. Three of the seven measured
// positions are NOT closed by this change and are declared with their triggers in the gap analysis
// (`docs/plans/compiler-guarantee-recovery-gap-analysis.md`): the variant POSITIONAL payload, the
// callable-type RETURN and the callable-type PARAMETER. Each still admits a kernel today. A reader
// who takes this file as the class's closure will build on a guarantee covering four positions of
// seven.
//
// THE ALIAS SECTION BELOW IS A SECOND DEFECT AT THE SAME SEAM, ADDED 2026-08-27, AND IT DOES NOT
// CORRECT ANYTHING ABOVE. Every claim above is about a declared type written as a DIRECT name and
// stays true as written. What the census never varied is the SPELLING of the declared type, and a
// bare `type KAlias = KOuter` declaration turned out to defeat the relation those probes exercise:
// it is neither Conj nor Disj and has no children of its own, because its structure lives on the
// declaration it names, so the judgment fell through to the admitting arm. `5` inhabited `KAlias`
// while the identical `5` was refused at `KOuter`, one name away.
//
// Measured on a binary built from committed main, one variable changed between the two:
//
//   alias over a record, record-field position   6 files emitted, 0 diagnostics   THE DEFECT
//   bare record, same position                   refused: expected Product, got Primitive
//
// THE ALIAS PROBES LIVE HERE RATHER THAN IN A CARRIER OF THEIR OWN because they are the same
// relation asked about the same question, and `kernel_in_named_field_still_refuses` above is
// already this seam's control. A second file would have been a second authority for one fact.
//
// WHAT THE SCOPE ACTUALLY WAS, because "aliases were unchecked" overstates it in one direction and
// understates it in another. An alias whose right-hand side is a KERNEL type
// (`type KIntAlias = Int`) was already covered, but not by this relation:
// `peel_nominal_alias_identity` drops the brand for a transparent primitive right-hand side, so the
// formal arrives spelled `Int` and never reaches the arm that failed. An alias over a RECORD or a
// COPRODUCT keeps its brand, arrives spelled `KAlias`, and was admitted -- at every position that
// consults the relation without peeling first. The record-literal field seam peels and was covered;
// `declared_type_inhabitance` (the four positions above) and `direct_call_arg_type_mismatch` pass
// the authored formal straight through and were not.
//
// THE REPAIR FOLLOWS THE ALIAS AND RE-ASKS THE SAME QUESTION rather than restating any part of it
// (DESIGN section 3): the relation re-enters itself at the alias target, so every exemption it
// already owns -- name equality, `dag_can_cast`, the structural-carrier template, the single-child
// refinement shape -- is carried to the followed name instead of being re-derived beside it. The
// walk is depth-bounded because `type A = B` / `type B = A` is authorable source; the bound falls to
// the admitting arm, which is where an unfollowable alias already landed, never to a fabricated
// verdict.
//
// WHAT THE ALIAS SECTION DOES NOT CLOSE, stated rather than implied covered, AND NARROWED BY A LATER
// MEASUREMENT SO IT IS NOT READ WIDER THAN IT IS. At the RECORD-FIELD position, a bare `5` at a
// container-typed field -- `subject: List<KOuter>` -- is still admitted, aliased or not. The
// discriminating control is `type KAliasList = List<KOuter>` against a bare `List<KOuter>` at that
// same position: BOTH admit, before and after the repair, so the alias is not the variable there.
//
// IT DOES NOT FOLLOW THAT CONTAINERS ARE UNJUDGED EVERYWHERE, and an earlier revision of this note
// invited that reading. At the DIRECT-CALL ARGUMENT position a String at `type OneArg = List<Int>`
// REFUSES on this branch, as does one at `type TwoArg = Map<String, Int>`. So the container shape is
// judged at one position and not at the other.
//
// TWO VARIABLES DIFFER BETWEEN THOSE TWO MEASUREMENTS -- the position (record field vs direct-call
// argument) AND the produced value (`5` vs a String) -- so which of them is decisive is NOT
// established here, and this note does not guess. What is established: the admitting case is a bare
// kernel at a container-typed RECORD FIELD, and it is not an alias defect.
//
// dissolve-on: none. Per DESIGN section 4b(4) the evidence stays enrolled when the class climbs --
// deleting it would recreate specification-without-execution one rung up.

fn kernel_inhabitance_fn_return_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module kin.ret\n\nimport std.types { Int }\n\ntype KInner { value: Int }\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped { inner: KInner }\n\nfn kin_ret() -> KOuter {\n  5\n}\n"
}

fn kernel_inhabitance_data_annotation_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module kin.data\n\nimport std.types { Int }\n\ntype KInner { value: Int }\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped { inner: KInner }\n\ndata kin_data: KOuter = 5\n"
}

fn kernel_inhabitance_annotated_let_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module kin.let\n\nimport std.types { Int }\n\ntype KInner { value: Int }\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped { inner: KInner }\n\nfn kin_let() -> Int {\n  let x: KOuter = 5\n  1\n}\n"
}

fn kernel_inhabitance_list_element_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module kin.list\n\nimport std.types { Int, List }\n\ntype KInner { value: Int }\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped { inner: KInner }\n\ndata kin_list: List<KOuter> = [5]\n"
}

fn kernel_inhabitance_named_field_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module kin.field\n\nimport std.types { Int }\n\ntype KInner { value: Int }\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped { inner: KInner }\n\ntype KHold { subject: KOuter }\n\nfn kin_field() -> KHold {\n  KHold { subject: 5 }\n}\n"
}

fn kernel_inhabitance_positive_control_source() -> String {
  "module kin.control\n\nimport std.types { Int, List }\n\ntype KInner { value: Int }\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped { inner: KInner }\n\nfn kin_wrapped() -> KOuter {\n  KWrapped { inner: KInner { value: 7 } }\n}\n\ndata kin_ok_data: KOuter = KWrapped { inner: KInner { value: 7 } }\n\ndata kin_ok_list: List<KOuter> = [KWrapped { inner: KInner { value: 7 } }]\n\nfn kin_ok_let() -> Int {\n  let x: KOuter = kin_wrapped()\n  1\n}\n"
}

// THE DECLARED RETURN. `5` is not a `KOuter` and there is no representation in between: the produced
// side is a ground kernel scalar, so a name difference here is a difference.
test fn kernel_where_coproduct_declared_return_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    kernel_inhabitance_fn_return_fixture_source(),
    "src/kin_ret.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE MODULE-SCOPE `data` ANNOTATION -- the seam the gap analysis filed as seam (3), unwalled.
test fn kernel_where_coproduct_declared_data_annotation_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    kernel_inhabitance_data_annotation_fixture_source(),
    "src/kin_data.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE ANNOTATED `let`. This position had NO conformance judgment at all before this change -- not a
// judgment that missed a shape, an absent one.
test fn kernel_where_coproduct_declared_annotated_let_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    kernel_inhabitance_annotated_let_fixture_source(),
    "src/kin_let.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE CONTAINER ELEMENT -- the seam the gap analysis ranked FIRST of the unwalled two, because a
// wrong element does not merely admit a bad value, it disables the comparison that would have caught
// it. The declared type is reached as a type ARGUMENT rather than as a declaration of its own, which
// is why nobody had named the position before the grammar census.
test fn kernel_where_coproduct_declared_list_element_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    kernel_inhabitance_list_element_fixture_source(),
    "src/kin_list.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE POSITION THAT WAS ALREADY CLOSED, enrolled as the discriminator that this change REUSES the
// existing relation rather than minting a second one beside it. If this reds, the record-literal
// field seam and the positions above have drifted apart and there are two authorities for one fact.
//
// ITS SCOPE IS THE DIRECT SPELLING, and saying so is the whole of the narrowing above applied to the
// one probe most likely to be cited as this seam's coverage. `KOuter` here is written as a direct
// name. Its aliased twin is `kernel_at_aliased_named_field_must_refuse` below, and until that probe
// existed the aliased spelling of this exact position admitted.
test fn kernel_in_named_field_still_refuses() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    kernel_inhabitance_named_field_fixture_source(),
    "src/kin_field.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE POSITIVE CONTROL, carrying the conforming spelling at all four closed positions at once.
test fn correct_value_at_every_closed_position_still_compiles() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    kernel_inhabitance_positive_control_source(),
    "src/kin_control.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

fn alias_inhabitance_fn_return_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module ka.ret\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KInner \{ value: Int \}\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \}\n\ntype KAlias = KOuter\n\nfn ka_ret() -> KAlias \{\n  5\n\}\n"
}

fn alias_inhabitance_data_annotation_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module ka.data\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KInner \{ value: Int \}\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \}\n\ntype KAlias = KOuter\n\ndata ka_data: KAlias = 5\n"
}

fn alias_inhabitance_named_field_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module ka.field\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KInner \{ value: Int \}\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \}\n\ntype KAlias = KOuter\n\ntype KHold \{ subject: KAlias \}\n\nfn ka_field() -> KHold \{\n  KHold \{ subject: 5 \}\n\}\n"
}

fn alias_inhabitance_direct_call_argument_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module ka.call\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KInner \{ value: Int \}\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \}\n\ntype KAlias = KOuter\n\nfn ka_take(subject: KAlias) -> Int \{\n  1\n\}\n\nfn ka_call() -> Int \{\n  ka_take(subject: 5)\n\}\n"
}

fn alias_inhabitance_chained_alias_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module ka.chain\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KInner \{ value: Int \}\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \}\n\ntype KAliasOne = KOuter\n\ntype KAliasTwo = KAliasOne\n\ntype KAliasThree = KAliasTwo\n\nfn ka_chain() -> KAliasThree \{\n  5\n\}\n"
}

fn applied_alias_inhabitance_named_field_fixture_source() -> String {
  "module ka.applied\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KGen2<T> \{ value: T \}\n\ntype KAliasG = KGen2<Int>\n\ntype KHoldAG \{ subject: KAliasG \}\n\nfn ka_applied() -> KHoldAG \{\n  KHoldAG \{ subject: 5 \}\n\}\n"
}

fn alias_inhabitance_positive_control_source() -> String {
  "module ka.control\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KInner \{ value: Int \}\n\ntype KOuter\n  = KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \}\n\ntype KAlias = KOuter\n\ntype KHold \{ subject: KAlias \}\n\nfn ka_wrapped() -> KAlias \{\n  KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \{ value: 7 \} \}\n\}\n\ndata ka_ok_data: KAlias = KWrapped \{ inner: KInner \{ value: 7 \} \}\n\nfn ka_ok_field() -> KHold \{\n  KHold \{ subject: ka_wrapped() \}\n\}\n"
}

fn alias_over_kernel_positive_control_source() -> String {
  "module ka.kernel\n\nimport std.types \{ Int \}\n\ntype KIntAlias = Int\n\ntype KIntHold \{ subject: KIntAlias \}\n\nfn ka_kernel_take(subject: KIntAlias) -> Int \{\n  subject\n\}\n\ndata ka_kernel_data: KIntAlias = 5\n\nfn ka_kernel_field() -> KIntHold \{\n  KIntHold \{ subject: ka_kernel_take(subject: 5) \}\n\}\n"
}

// THE DECLARED RETURN, through one alias. `5` is not a `KOuter`, and spelling `KOuter` as `KAlias`
// does not change what the value is.
test fn kernel_at_aliased_declared_return_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    alias_inhabitance_fn_return_fixture_source(),
    "src/ka_ret.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE MODULE-SCOPE `data` ANNOTATION, through one alias.
test fn kernel_at_aliased_data_annotation_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    alias_inhabitance_data_annotation_fixture_source(),
    "src/ka_data.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE RECORD-LITERAL FIELD, through one alias. This is the seam that was ALREADY closed for the
// un-aliased spelling -- `KHold \{ subject: 5 \}` at a bare `KOuter` refuses today -- so its aliased
// twin is the sharpest available statement of the defect: two spellings of one declared type, one
// refused and one admitted, at the identical position.
test fn kernel_at_aliased_named_field_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    alias_inhabitance_named_field_fixture_source(),
    "src/ka_field.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE DIRECT-CALL ARGUMENT, through one alias -- the second position that passes the authored formal
// straight through without peeling, and so the second one the defect reached.
test fn kernel_at_aliased_direct_call_argument_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    alias_inhabitance_direct_call_argument_fixture_source(),
    "src/ka_call.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// DEPTH IS NOT SPECIAL-CASED, and this is the probe that says so. The repair re-enters the same
// relation rather than peeling a fixed number of levels, so a three-link alias chain is decided by
// the same code as a one-link one. A repair that peeled once would pass the four probes above and
// red here.
test fn kernel_at_chained_alias_declared_return_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    alias_inhabitance_chained_alias_fixture_source(),
    "src/ka_chain.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE POSITIVE CONTROL. Without it a relation that refused every aliased declaration would satisfy
// all five reds above, and the reds would measure the mechanism rather than the distinction.
test fn conforming_value_through_an_alias_still_compiles() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    alias_inhabitance_positive_control_source(),
    "src/ka_control.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE EXEMPTION CONTROL, and it is the one that would catch the likeliest way to get this wrong. The
// repair follows the alias and then re-applies the relation's own exemptions at the followed name;
// a repair that followed the alias and then judged on the DECLARATION SHAPE alone would newly refuse
// an alias over a kernel type, where the value is perfectly good. Every position the reds above
// cover is carried here with a conforming value at `type KIntAlias = Int`.
test fn kernel_value_at_an_alias_over_that_kernel_still_compiles() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    alias_over_kernel_positive_control_source(),
    "src/ka_kernel.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}

// THE APPLIED ALIAS -- `type KAliasG = KGen2<Int>`, an alias whose right-hand side is a type
// APPLICATION rather than a bare name. It is enrolled separately from the bare-name probes above
// because its binding has a different shape: it arrives carrying a CHILD (the argument) where a
// bare-name alias arrives with none, so a repair that keyed on childlessness would pass every probe
// above and red here. Measured: admits before the repair, refuses after.
test fn kernel_at_applied_alias_named_field_must_refuse() -> Bool {
  !compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    applied_alias_inhabitance_named_field_fixture_source(),
    "src/ka_applied.rs",
    [],
    []
  )
}
